      "code": "2.3",
      "title": "Projectile Motion, Part 1: Horizontal Launch",
      "concept": {
        "summary": "A horizontally launched projectile leaves with all of its velocity sideways and none of it up or down. Gravity pulls only downward, so the two directions are solved separately. Vertically it is free fall from rest (S1.4). Horizontally it moves at constant velocity. The two motions share one thing: the time.",
        "keyIdeas": [
          "The horizontal speed never changes in the air. The vertical speed starts at 0 and grows by 9.8 m/s every second.",
          "Time is the bridge. Whichever direction gives you the time, carry it over to the other one."
        ]
      },
      "priorKnowledge": {
        "items": [
          "Free fall: the acceleration is g = 9.8 m/s² downward, whatever the object is doing (S1.4).",
          "Constant velocity: distance = velocity × time (S1.1).",
          "Constant acceleration: v² = v0² + 2a·d (S1.2)."
        ]
      },
      "directions": "Work every problem in your Hayden-McNeil notebook. Sketch the path first and label what you know. Up is +y and the direction of launch is +x, so g = −9.8 m/s² and a landing spot below the launch point has a negative y. Every object here leaves moving horizontally, so its starting vertical velocity is vy0 = 0. Ignore air resistance. Round answers to the correct number of significant figures; g = 9.8 m/s² has two.",
